<article class="env-lemma" id="07K2">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07K2">Lemma <span data-tag="07K2">10.43.7</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field and let $S$ be a $k$-algebra. Assume that $S$ is reduced and that $S_{\mathfrak p}$ is geometrically reduced for every minimal prime $\mathfrak p$ of $S$. Then $S$ is geometrically reduced. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since $S$ is reduced the map $S \to \prod _{\mathfrak p\text{ minimal}} S_{\mathfrak p}$ is injective, see Lemma <a href="/tag/00EW" data-tag="00EW">10.25.2</a>. If $K/k$ is a field extension, then the maps 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  S \otimes _ k K \to (\prod S_\mathfrak p) \otimes _ k K \to \prod S_\mathfrak p \otimes _ k K  \]
</div>
    <p>
       are injective: the first as $k \to K$ is flat and the second by inspection because $K$ is a free $k$-module. As $S_\mathfrak p$ is geometrically reduced the ring on the right is reduced. Thus we see that $S \otimes _ k K$ is reduced as a subring of a reduced ring. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>